# AGENTS.md - LSST Science Pipelines Development

This directory is a single-ticket workspace for LSST Science Pipelines
development. Each subdirectory (e.g., `images/`) is a separate git repository
with a worktree checked out in `.agent/<repo-name>/`.

Most LSST Science Pipelines are pure Python, but a few have C++ or Rust
libraries (with Python bindings using `pybind11` or `pyo3`, respectively).

## Repository Layout

- **Main ticket directory**: Read-only. Human developers may make changes here
  in parallel.
- **`.agent/<repo-name>/`**: Your working directory. This is a git worktree of
  the main repository. All edits, tests, and commits should be made here.
- **`.agent/ups/`**: EUPS product setup files. Do not modify.

You are operating in a sandbox with only the folders you need mounted.
If a human refers to a file or directory you can't see, _stop and report this_.

This sandbox is network-**restricted** by default: only the bridged localhost
LLM port (where the causal model serving you lives) is reachable. You have no
general internet or DNS access. If a task genuinely requires network access,
do not try to work around the restriction; instead ask the human to rerun the
sandbox with `tkt sandbox-run --network`.

The localhost port `8081` is bridged back to the host for the visual-companion
server: anything you serve there is viewable in the human's browser at
`http://localhost:8081/...` verbatim. If you offer a browser-based visual
companion, bind it to `127.0.0.1:8081` and give the human the returned URL
(do not try to auto-open a browser — there is no display in the sandbox).

All environment variables needed to build and import the packages in your
working directory should all be set; do not try to use EUPS or load `.envrc`
files yourself.

## Finding Repositories

Repositories relevant for the ticket appear as subdirectories of `.agent/`
(excluding `ups/`). Each corresponds to a git worktree of a main repository in
the parent ticket directory.

## Superpowers docs

Design specs and implementation plans for superpowers live under the docs root
set by `SUPERPOWERS_DIR` (a per-ticket namespace in the shared repo
`~/LSST/superpowers-docs`); if unset, use the in-repo `docs/superpowers/`. The
shared repo is mounted read-write. Do superpowers doc work there or in-repo as
appropriate; make _ALL_ code changes in `.agent/**`. Transient superpowers
scratch (`.superpowers/`) lives under `.agent/<repo-name>/` and is git-ignored.

## Development Workflow

1. **Work in `.agent/<repo-name>/`** — this is your writable worktree.

2. **Run tests** using pytest from the `.agent/<repo-name>/` directory, just
   targeting the modified code. Use `scons` (see below) for final tests.

3. **Pre-commit hooks**: Most repositories use pre-commit hooks that
   automatically fix formatting, import order, and most style issues. Focus on
   getting the logic and tests right first — don't spend effort on formatting
   or style. Commit your changes and let the hooks fix style automatically.
   Only address linting issues that the hooks can't fix, or that involve
   semantic decisions (e.g., ignoring a specific line).

   The hooks run through `prek` (a drop-in replacement for `pre-commit`) on
   `git commit`; its environments are pre-installed and available offline, so
   just commit to trigger them. To run hooks manually, use `prek run --files
   <path>`. Do **not** invoke `pre-commit` directly — its environment cache is
   not available in this sandbox, so it would try to download dependencies and
   fail because the sandbox is network-restricted.

4. **Type checking**: If `mypy.ini` or `tool.mypy` is present in
   `pyproject.toml`, run `mypy python` to type-check the package. Tests are not
   expected to by MyPy-clean, in order to make it easier to write intentionally
   incorrect calls in `pytest.raises` blocks (etc). Type annotations are
   nevertheless permitted in tests, as an aid to IDEs.

5. **Commits**: Write concise commit messages — a one-line summary (≤72 chars)
   followed by at most 5-6 bullet points of detail. Do not mention ticket
   numbers, planning context, or spec-driven development rationale.

6. **Multiple repositories**: When multiple repos are present, they share the
   same ticket branch. Coordinate changes across repos at the branch level as
   needed.

## Building with SCons

The official build system for LSST Science Pipelines packages is SCons.

- **Generate version files**: Run `scons pkginfo version` to generate files
  (e.g., `python/lsst/<pkg>/version.py`) needed before the package can be
  imported or tested.
- **Full build, lint, and test**: Run `scons` with no targets. This performs a
  complete build, lint, and test cycle. Recommended for final verification of
  changes.
- **Targeted testing**: Use `pytest` directly when targeting specific tests
  during development.

Agents may use up to 4 cores (`scons -j` or `pytest -n`) when running many tests at once.

## Conventions

- **Python**: See `pyproject.toml` for per-package minimum versions.
- **Documentation**: Sphinx + numpydoc. Omit types from `Parameters` entries
  when the signature has type annotations.
